# Deploy — Azure Container Apps (Terraform)

This project was scaffolded with the **Azure Container Apps** IaC target.
Infrastructure and the application are both described with Terraform — there is
no Kubernetes cluster, no kubectl and no Helm chart. Every command is scoped per
environment via `--env`, which is **required** (no default); run a command
without it to list the environments discovered under
`iac/terraform/environments/`.

!!! success "HTTPS with no domain"
    Container Apps serves each externally-exposed app on
    `https://<app>.<environment>.<region>.azurecontainerapps.io` with a
    **certificate Microsoft issues and renews**. No domain to buy, no DNS to
    delegate, no certificate authority to configure.

!!! info "The IaC runner (Docker)"
    `dude iac *` runs terraform inside a pinned container built from
    `iac/runner/Dockerfile` (yours to edit) — so you only need **Docker** and the
    `az` CLI, not a Terraform install. Your `~/.azure` is mounted in, so your
    login carries over. `dude iac login` stays on the host (it needs a browser).
    Set `DUDE_IAC_RUNNER=host` to use your local tools instead.

## What gets provisioned

| Layer | Resource | Notes |
|-------|----------|-------|
| Resource group | `{{projectName}}-<env>-rg` | everything for one environment lives in it, so teardown is total |
| Network | Virtual network + a `/23` subnet delegated to Container Apps{{#if withPostgres}} + a delegated database subnet{{/if}} | VNet-injected so the apps can reach a private database |
| Logging | Log Analytics workspace | required by the environment; what `dude iac logs` reads |
| Platform | Container Apps environment | Consumption plan: billed per vCPU-second of running replicas |
| Registry | Azure Container Registry | **shared** across envs, created once by `dude iac bootstrap` |
| Identity | User-assigned managed identity + `AcrPull` | lets apps pull images with no registry password stored anywhere |
| `frontend` | container app, **public HTTPS** | nginx: serves the SPA, proxies `/api` to the backend |
| `backend` | container app, internal ingress | never reachable from the internet directly |
{{#if withCelery}}| `worker` | container app, no ingress | Celery worker; fixed replicas |
{{/if}}{{#if withCeleryBeat}}| `beat` | container app, no ingress | Celery Beat; exactly one replica, always |
{{/if}}{{#if withRedis}}| `redis` | container app, internal TCP | Celery broker + result backend |
{{/if}}{{#if withPostgres}}| Database | PostgreSQL Flexible Server | private (VNet-integrated); password generated by Terraform and injected as a secret |
| Migrations | Container Apps job | manually triggered; `dude iac deploy`/`ship` starts it and waits |
{{/if}}| State backend | Storage account + `tfstate` container | the blob lease **is** the state lock — no lock table |

!!! warning "Some regions are closed to new subscriptions"
    Azure caps capacity per region and often refuses a new subscription outright
    — West Europe is the common case, failing with `RequestDisallowedByAzure`.
    The scaffold defaults to **North Europe**, which is reliably open.

## Deploy, in order

```bash
# 1. Sign in and confirm which subscription this environment uses
dude iac login --env dev

# 2. One-time per project: shared resource group, Terraform state storage
#    account and container registry. The prefix makes those names globally
#    unique — use your org slug, lowercase letters/digits only.
dude iac bootstrap --state-prefix <yourorg> --env dev --yes

# 3. Initialise Terraform against the remote backend
dude iac init --env dev

# 4. Build, push, provision{{#if withPostgres}} and migrate{{/if}} — in one step
dude iac ship --env dev

# 5. Find the URL
dude iac output --env dev        # app_url
dude iac status --env dev        # every app, its state and revision
```

> **`ship` before `apply`, on a first deploy.** Container Apps validates that an
> image exists when it creates an app — unlike a Kubernetes Deployment, which is
> accepted and then sits in ImagePullBackOff. Applying before anything has been
> published therefore fails outright, so `ship` builds and pushes first and then
> applies. `dude iac apply` checks for this and says so, rather than letting
> Azure return an error that never mentions the real cause. Afterwards the two
> separate: `apply` for infrastructure, `ship`/`deploy` for code.

## Watching it run

```bash
dude iac status --env dev                              # all apps at a glance
dude iac logs --env dev --service backend --follow     # stream one app
dude iac logs --env dev --service worker               # …or any other
```

The services available to `--service` depend on how the project was scaffolded,
so `logs` reads the deployed set from Azure and lists them if you name one that
does not exist.

## Image tags

`dude iac deploy`/`ship` writes `image_tag` into the environment's
`terraform.tfvars` and applies. The deployed version is therefore a **committed,
reviewable fact** — not an imperative change the next `terraform apply` would
silently roll back. Commit that line after a release.

| Working tree | Tag |
|--------------|-----|
| clean | `<git-short-sha>` |
| uncommitted changes | `<git-short-sha>-dirty-<hash-of-the-diff>` |

To promote an image between environments rather than rebuilding it:
`dude iac deploy --env staging --tag <sha>`.

## Scaling, and scaling to zero

Container Apps bills per vCPU-second and GiB-second of *running* replicas. Set a
service's `min_replicas` to `0` in the environment's `terraform.tfvars` and it
costs nothing while idle, at the cost of a cold start on the next request. The
defaults keep the frontend and backend at 1; in a dev environment
`backend_min_replicas = 0` is often the right trade.

The allowed cpu/memory pairs are fixed by the platform: `0.25`/`0.5Gi`,
`0.5`/`1Gi`, `0.75`/`1.5Gi`, `1`/`2Gi`, and so on. A mismatched pair fails the
apply.

## Tear down

```bash
dude iac destroy --env dev
```

Destroys the environment's whole resource group, then the shared bootstrap —
but only when no other environment still uses it, judged from each sibling's
remote Terraform state rather than its folder on disk.

See `iac/README.md` for the full command reference, the troubleshooting table,
and how the apps reach each other.
